script:
{
  "topic": "Sudden job loss and living in a car",
  "title": "POV: You Had a Good Job and Now You Live in Your Car",
  "sections": [
    {
      "name": "Level 1: The Catalyst",
      "segments": [
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: First-person POV of hands holding a standard corporate ID badge. The light on the scanner next to an office door is blinking red. The door is closed.",
          "narration": "The red light blinks twice. Access Denied. You try it again, a little faster this time, as if speed could change the outcome. Red again. It's 8:57 AM on a Tuesday. For six years, that green light was a starting gun you never even thought about. Today, it's a stop sign. A security guard you've never seen before walks towards you. He doesn't look angry, just tired. 'Morning, sir. Can you come with me, please?'",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: Extreme close-up on a single cardboard box on an empty office desk. Inside: a coffee mug with a faded logo, a phone charger, a framed photo from a fishing trip, and a generic black stapler.",
          "narration": "Six years fits into one cardboard box. They call it 'restructuring'. Your boss, who took you golfing last month, can't quite meet your eye. He says words like 'synergy' and 'headwinds'. You're not really listening. You're looking at this box. A mug, a photo, a charger, and a stapler you're pretty sure was never yours. This is the official inventory of a career that ended three minutes ago. You're walked out past your team, who are all pretending to be incredibly busy.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: POV shot from the driver's seat of a 2015 sedan. The cardboard box is on the passenger seat. The office building is visible in the rearview mirror, shrinking as you drive away.",
          "narration": "You sit in your car in the parking lot for nineteen minutes. You're not sad. You're not angry. You're just... blank. The engine is running. The AC is on. You look at the box on the passenger seat. It feels like an alien object. You finally put the car in drive, pulling out of the parking spot you've used for 2,190 days. You don't know where you're going, so you just drive towards home. Or what used to be.",
          "pause": true,
          "twist": false
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "Level 2: The Descent",
      "segments": [
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: A tired man in his 30s with a grey hoodie sits on a couch in a sparsely furnished apartment. He's staring at a laptop screen, which illuminates his face in the dim room.",
          "narration": "That night, you open the laptop. The severance is two weeks' pay. A gesture, they called it. You do the math. Then you do it again, hoping for a different result. The numbers are simple. The numbers are brutal. They don't care about your feelings. They just are. You pull up your banking app. It's a four-digit number you used to think was a decent cushion. Now it looks like a countdown timer.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "graphic",
          "visual": "graphic: BANK BALANCE: $7,450. SEVERANCE: $2,100. MONTHLY RENT: $1,450.",
          "narration": "Your checking account holds $7,450. The severance adds $2,100, totaling $9,550. Your rent is $1,450 a month, due in nine days. Your car payment is $380. Insurance, $150. Utilities, another $200. That's $2,180 a month just to exist in a place you're now only visiting. You realize, with a cold, sinking feeling, that you have about four months. Four months until zero. If you're lucky.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: POV shot of hands typing 'how to get out of a lease early' into a search engine on the laptop. The search results are a wall of blue links about penalties and legal fees.",
          "narration": "The first casualty is the lease. You spend two hours reading about termination clauses and penalties. It would cost you two months' rent to leave. That's $2,900. An impossible number. So you make a decision. A cold, pragmatic decision that feels like a surrender. You're not going to pay next month's rent. You're going to take the hit to your credit. You're going to use that money to survive what comes next. The eviction notice won't come for weeks. That's time.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: Wide shot of your sedan parked on a quiet suburban street at dusk. You're transferring black trash bags filled with clothes from your apartment into the trunk of your car.",
          "narration": "You spend the next three weeks selling your life, piece by piece. The TV, the couch, the coffee table. You keep your clothes, your laptop, and your documents. The day before the first of the month, you pack the last of it into your car. The apartment feels vast and empty. You do a final walkthrough, a ghost in your own home. You drop the keys on the counter and walk out without looking back. You don't have a destination. You just have a vehicle.",
          "pause": true,
          "twist": false
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "Level 3: The Unspoken Rules",
      "segments": [
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: POV shot from inside the car, looking out the windshield at a 24-hour Walmart parking lot at night. The neon sign glows. A few other scattered cars are also parked and dark.",
          "narration": "The first night, you park in a Walmart lot. It feels logical. It's open 24/7, there's light, there are other cars. You recline the driver's seat. It's not comfortable. Every sound is magnified. A shopping cart rattling, a car alarm in the distance, the hum of the highway. You don't sleep. You just lie there, waiting for the one sound you dread: a knock on the window. This is your new reality. A glass box in a sea of asphalt.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: Close-up on a smartphone screen displaying a map of the local area. Red pins are dropped on locations like 'Planet Fitness', 'Public Library', and 'Industrial Park'.",
          "narration": "You quickly learn the first rule: routine is survival. You buy a Planet Fitness Black Card for $25 a month. It's your new bathroom, your shower, your moment of quiet dignity. The library is your new office. Free Wi-Fi, a place to sit for hours without being questioned. You spend your days applying for jobs online, hundreds of them. The rejection emails pile up, each one a small, digital papercut.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "graphic",
          "visual": "graphic: MONTHLY COST OF SURVIVAL: Gas: $150. Gym: $25. Phone: $34. Storage Unit: $70.",
          "narration": "You learn the math of the street. You get a small 5x10 storage unit for $70 a month. It holds the things you couldn't bear to sell. You learn to live on gas station coffee and cheap groceries. A loaf of bread, peanut butter, bananas. You track every penny. The goal is to keep your burn rate under $800 a month. Your savings account is a melting iceberg, and you're watching it disappear one drip at a time.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: A police cruiser's headlights illuminate the interior of your car. From the driver's seat POV, you see the officer's flashlight beam dance across your face.",
          "narration": "It happens on your third week. The Knock. 2 AM, in a quiet park you thought was safe. A flashlight beam cuts through your window shades. Your heart hammers against your ribs. You roll the window down a few inches. The cop is young. He's not aggressive, just firm. 'You can't sleep here, man.' He tells you to move along. He doesn't tell you where to go. That's the real message. 'We don't want you here.'",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: Close-up on your hands carefully fitting custom-cut black foam core panels into the rear windows of the car. It's precise, leaving no gaps for light to enter or escape.",
          "narration": "That's when you learn Rule #2: Be invisible. You buy black foam board and cut it perfectly to fit your windows. From the outside, it just looks like dark tint. You never park in the same spot two nights in a row. You learn to blend in. Hospital parking lots, industrial parks, apartment complexes where you're just another car. You arrive late, you leave early. You become a ghost.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: The tired man in his 30s with a grey hoodie is sitting in the driver's seat, eating a sandwich out of a plastic bag. He stares blankly out the windshield at a brick wall.",
          "narration": "The loneliness is a physical weight. You talk to no one. The cashier at the grocery store, the front desk person at the gym. These are your only human interactions. You see families walking in the park, couples having dinner in restaurants. They live in a world you used to belong to. A world that is now separated from you by a pane of glass.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: Over-the-shoulder shot of the man looking at his laptop. An email is on the screen with the subject line: 'Thank you for your application...'",
          "narration": "You get a callback for an interview. A job you're overqualified for, but it's a lifeline. You iron your shirt in the laundromat. You use the library bathroom to shave. You walk into the office feeling like an impostor. The interview goes well. They say they'll be in touch. They never are. The hope makes the failure feel ten times worse. Self-pity is a luxury you cannot afford.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: POV shot looking down at a cracked smartphone screen. The banking app shows a balance of $812.15.",
          "narration": "Two months in, the reality of your situation solidifies. The job applications have yielded nothing. The savings are almost gone. $812. The number stares back at you, an indictment. You've been falling, but you didn't realize how fast. Now, you can see the ground. You've been surviving, but you haven't been living. And surviving is about to get a lot harder.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: A wide shot of an industrial area at dawn. Several older, beat-up cars are parked in a line near a warehouse. It looks cold.",
          "narration": "You start noticing the other ghosts. The old van with the curtains always drawn. The station wagon with clothes piled high in the back. You exchange a silent nod with a woman in an SUV at the gym. You both know why you're really there at 5:30 AM. There's no community, just a shared, unspoken understanding. A quiet fraternity of the downwardly mobile.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: First-person POV of hands gripping the steering wheel tightly, knuckles white. The car is parked, and rain is streaking down the windshield, blurring the outside world.",
          "narration": "You learn the final rule of this level. Despair is the only thing out here that will actually kill you. It's a predator that stalks the quiet moments. The moments between turning off the engine and trying to sleep. You fight it with podcasts, with audiobooks, with anything to fill the silence. You can't afford to let it in. Not yet. You have to believe there's another level to this game.",
          "pause": true,
          "twist": false
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "Level 4: The Bottom & The Pivot",
      "segments": [
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: A close-up on the gas gauge in your car dashboard. The needle is hovering just above 'E'. A small orange warning light is on.",
          "narration": "The day comes when the math finally runs out. Your bank balance is $47. Gas is $3.80 a gallon. Your tank is on empty. This is it. The abstract fear of 'running out of money' is now a concrete reality. You can't drive to the library. You can't charge your phone. You are stranded. This, you realize, is the bottom. A quiet parking lot, a silent engine, and forty-seven dollars to your name.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: POV shot of hands holding a smartphone, looking at a map. A pin is dropped on a location labeled 'Labor Ready' or a similar day labor office.",
          "narration": "There's a place you've driven past a dozen times. A day labor office. At 5 AM, there's always a line of men standing outside, waiting. You always looked away, a flicker of pity mixed with a thankfulness that it wasn't you. Now, it is you. The thought is humiliating. It feels like a final admission of defeat. But defeat is a feeling. Hunger is a fact.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: A line of diverse, tired-looking men standing outside a plain storefront before sunrise. The tired man in his 30s with a grey hoodie is at the back of the line, looking at the ground.",
          "narration": "You use your last few dollars for gas and drive there the next morning. It's 4:45 AM. You're the 20th person in line. Nobody talks. You stand in the cold, a former account manager in a line with construction workers and guys who look like they've been doing this for years. You feel their eyes on you. You're the new guy. The soft one. You just stare at your shoes and wait.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: Close-up on a pair of worn, dirty leather work gloves being pulled onto your clean, soft hands. The contrast is stark.",
          "narration": "Your name gets called at 6:15. A landscaping job. Unloading mulch from a truck. The pay is minimum wage, minus their fee. Cash at the end of the day. A man hands you a pair of gloves. They're stiff and smell like sweat and dirt. You put them on. The work is brutal. Your back aches. Your hands are raw. You're working alongside a man named Carlos who doesn't speak much English, but shows you how to lift without breaking your back.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "graphic",
          "visual": "graphic: DAY'S WAGES: $92.00. LUNCH: $8.50. GAS: $15.00. NET: $68.50.",
          "narration": "At 4 PM, the foreman pays you. $92 in cash. It feels like a fortune. You've never worked so hard for so little money in your life. But it's real. It's in your hand. This is the pivot. The moment you stop falling and start climbing. You buy a hot meal for the first time in weeks. It tastes like victory. The math has changed. You're no longer just spending. You're earning.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: The man is in his car at night, meticulously writing in a small notebook with a pen. He's surrounded by the foam-core window panels.",
          "narration": "This becomes your new routine. Up at 4 AM. The day labor line. Work whatever job you can get. Landscaping, demolition, moving furniture. Every evening, you go to the gym to shower off the grime. Then you park, eat your cold dinner, and write down every dollar earned and spent in a notebook. You become ruthlessly disciplined. There is no room for error.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: First-person POV of hands sorting a small pile of cash on the passenger seat. A few twenties, some tens, fives, and ones. It's worn and crumpled.",
          "narration": "You learn the hierarchy of the line. The skilled guys - the painters, the carpenters - they get picked first. The general labor guys like you are last. You start paying attention. You learn how to tape drywall from watching a guy on a lunch break. You buy a cheap tape measure and a utility knife. You start telling the dispatcher you have experience. Sometimes, it works.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: A shot from behind the tired man in his 30s as he sits in the library, but this time he's not on job sites. He's watching a YouTube tutorial on 'how to install flooring'.",
          "narration": "The library is no longer for job applications. It's for learning skills. You watch videos on tiling, on basic plumbing, on electrical work. You're not just trying to survive the day anymore. You're trying to increase your value for the next day. You learn that a guy who can lay vinyl plank flooring makes $18 an hour, not $12. The climb is measured in dollars per hour.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: Close-up on the notebook. One page has a line graph drawn by hand, showing daily earnings. The line is jagged, but trending slowly, almost imperceptibly, upwards.",
          "narration": "After a month of this, you have a small breakthrough. A contractor you've worked for a few times likes your attitude. He asks if you want to work for him directly for a few weeks, off the books. Consistent work. A little more pay. It's the first rung on a new ladder. You say yes without hesitation. For the first time in a long time, there's a fragile, unfamiliar feeling in your chest. It's called momentum.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: Your car is parked in a 'Safe Parking' lot, designated by a church or community center. Other cars are there. It's organized and feels less precarious than a random street.",
          "narration": "You hear about a 'Safe Parking' program run by a local church. You apply. There's a waiting list, but a spot opens up. It's a designated, secure lot where you can park overnight without fear of the knock. There's a portable toilet and a security guard. It's not a home, but it's a base. It's a small island of stability in an ocean of uncertainty. You can finally breathe. You can finally plan past sunrise.",
          "pause": true,
          "twist": false
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "Level 5: The Escalation",
      "segments": [
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: The man is on a construction site, wearing a hard hat and tool belt. He's focused, measuring a board. He looks tired but competent. The grey hoodie is gone, replaced by work clothes.",
          "narration": "The temporary gig with the contractor turns into a steady thing. His name is Frank. He's tough but fair. He sees you don't cut corners. You're always 15 minutes early. You learn fast. You start as a general laborer, but soon you're doing drywall finishing, then flooring. You're making $20 an hour. Cash. You're still living in your car, but now the math is different. You're not just surviving; you're saving.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "graphic",
          "visual": "graphic: WEEKLY INCOME: $800. WEEKLY EXPENSES: $150. WEEKLY SAVINGS: $650.",
          "narration": "Your life is a machine of pure efficiency. Your weekly expenses are still brutally low. You eat for less than $10 a day. Your only luxuries are the gym membership and a weekly trip to the laundromat. You're saving over $600 a week. The number in your secret savings envelope, tucked inside your car's spare tire well, starts to grow. First $1,000, then $3,000, then $5,000.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: The tired man, looking less tired now, sits in his car at night. He's eating from a Tupperware container, looking at real estate listings for small studio apartments on his phone.",
          "narration": "You start to allow yourself to think about the future again. A future with walls. You scroll through listings for small, cheap apartments. You do the math. First month's rent, last month's, security deposit. You'd need at least $4,000 to get in the door. A year ago, that was a weekend trip. Now, it's the summit of a mountain you've been climbing inch by inch. But you can see it. It's within reach.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: An Amazon delivery van pulls up on a residential street. The driver's face is visible, and it's a new character - a young woman looking stressed.",
          "narration": "One day on a job site, you see an Amazon driver. She looks exhausted, rushing to make her next stop. You overhear her talking to a homeowner, something about her rent going up again. She says she's working 60 hours a week and can barely make ends meet. She gets back in her van and drives off. You watch her go, and a strange thought hits you.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": true
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: A wide shot of the city skyline at dusk. The lights are coming on in thousands of windows in thousands of apartments.",
          "narration": "Here is the twist. The system isn't just crushing the unemployed. It's crushing the employed, too. That driver has a job. You have a job. You're both working, contributing. But the cost of entry, the price of a simple roof over your head, has become a luxury good. The assumption was that getting a job would solve your problem. The terrifying new reality is that a job is no longer the solution.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: POV shot of your hands holding the thick envelope of cash. You're in your car, counting it. The stack is significant now.",
          "narration": "You realize you've been playing the wrong game. The goal isn't just to get a job and an apartment. That's a trap. One medical bill, one car repair, one missed paycheck, and you're right back in this car. You're looking at your savings, over $8,000 now. You could get an apartment tomorrow. But you don't. The fear is too strong. The memory of having zero is burned into your brain.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: Close up on a laptop screen showing a stock trading interface. A simple index fund chart is displayed, showing slow, steady growth.",
          "narration": "You're not just escaping homelessness anymore. You're escaping the entire system of wage dependency that put you here. The library is now your financial classroom. You're not learning a trade. You're learning about index funds, about emergency funds, about building a buffer so thick that no single event can ever knock you down to zero again. Your car is no longer a prison. It's a tool. A savings machine.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: The man sits in his car in the safe parking lot, but now the interior is more organized. There are storage containers, a small cooler. It's a living space, not just a shelter.",
          "narration": "You upgrade your setup. A better sleeping pad. A small, high-efficiency cooler. A power inverter to charge your laptop without running the engine. You are a professional now. You've streamlined the process of living in a vehicle. It's not comfortable, but it's optimized. You are no longer being crushed by the system. You are exploiting a loophole in it.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: Frank, the contractor, is talking to you on a job site. He's gesturing at a set of blueprints, explaining something. You're nodding, understanding.",
          "narration": "Frank offers you a promotion. He wants you to be a site foreman. A real salary. A W-2. Health insurance. It's everything you dreamed of six months ago. It means more responsibility, longer hours. It means you'll have less time, but more money. The old you would have jumped at it. The new you hesitates. You ask for a day to think about it. You need to do the math.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: You're back in the library, but now you're on a government website, looking up how to register a business. 'LLC Formation', 'Sole Proprietorship'.",
          "narration": "You realize a salary is just a different kind of dependency. You've learned a trade. You're good at it. You have your own tools now, bought one by one with cash. Instead of taking the foreman job, you make Frank a counteroffer. You'll work for him as an independent contractor. You'll get your own insurance, handle your own taxes. He's surprised, but he agrees. It's a risk. It's also freedom.",
          "pause": true,
          "twist": false
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "Level 6: The Reversal",
      "segments": [
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: A stack of business cards on the passenger seat. The name is simple, 'Your Last Name - Home Repair'. A phone number is listed below.",
          "narration": "You are now a business. It's just you, your tools, and your car. You're a licensed and insured contractor. Frank is your first and biggest client, but he's not your only one. You post flyers. You get referrals. You're working 70 hours a week, but it's for yourself. The money is coming in faster now. The fear is still there, a constant hum beneath the surface, but now it's a motivator, not a paralytic.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "graphic",
          "visual": "graphic: BANK BALANCE: $22,150. INVESTMENTS: $5,000. DEBT: $0.",
          "narration": "A year after you were fired, you look at your finances. You have over $20,000 in the bank. You paid off your car. You have no debt. You have a small investment account. You're still living in your car. It's a conscious choice now. Every month you don't pay rent is another $1,500 added to your freedom fund. The math has been reversed. The car is no longer a symbol of poverty; it's a symbol of your savings rate.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: The man stands in a Home Depot aisle, looking at high-end power tools. He's no longer just surviving, he's investing in his business and capability.",
          "narration": "You start to see the world differently. You see waste everywhere. People paying for things they don't need, to impress people they don't know. You used to be one of them. Now, every purchase is a calculation. Will this make me money? Will this improve my efficiency? You buy a new, expensive drill. Not because it's flashy, but because it will save you 10 minutes per job, and time is money.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: You're at the day labor line at 5 AM, but you're not in the line. You're the one in the pickup truck, looking for two guys to help you with a big job.",
          "narration": "One morning, you find yourself on the other side of the day labor line. You need two guys for a demo job. You pull up in your own work truck, a used but reliable pickup you paid for in cash. You look at the line of men, their faces etched with the same tired hope you once had. You know exactly what they're feeling. You pick two guys, and you make sure to pay them a little more than the going rate.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: You're talking to a new, younger guy on your work crew. He looks lost. You're showing him the right way to hold a tool, patiently.",
          "narration": "One of the guys you hire, a kid named Leo, he reminds you of yourself. He's smart, just down on his luck. You see him making the same mistakes you made. You take him under your wing. You teach him how to finish drywall. You give him steady work when you have it. You're not just building a business. You're rewriting your own past, one small act at a time.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: POV shot looking at a real estate app again. But this time, you're not looking at cheap studios. You're looking at small houses, with 'For Sale' listings.",
          "narration": "The idea of renting starts to feel like a bad investment. Why pay someone else's mortgage? You've saved aggressively. You have a down payment. Not just for an apartment, but for a small house. A fixer-upper. Something you can pour your own sweat equity into. The scale of your ambition has shifted. You're not just trying to get back to where you were. You're trying to build something that can never be taken away.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
        },
        {
          "type": "broll",
          "visual": "broll: You're meeting with a real estate agent outside a small, slightly run-down single-family home. The 'For Sale' sign is in the yard.",
          "narration": "You find a house. It's small. It needs a new roof and a new kitchen. Other buyers see a wreck. You see an opportunity. You know exactly how much it will cost to fix, because you can do all the work yourself. You make an offer. A lowball offer, but it's all cash. The seller hesitates. But cash is king. They accept. You are no longer homeless. You are a homeowner.",
          "pause": false,
          "twist": false
